\subsection{Header and footer lines}{

DOCX and PDF files show the \code{header} and \code{footer} lines of the document (see
\code{\link[=pub_doc]{pub_doc()}}):
\itemize{
\item \code{{trace}} in a line is replaced by a line that says who rendered the report and
when, e.g. "Jane Doe at 07-Oct-2026 12:00:00 UTC (myapp v1.2.0)" (see
\code{\link[=pub_trace_stamp]{pub_trace_stamp()}}).
\item The footer gets "Page x / N" at the right unless the \code{footer} has a \code{right} part.
}
}

\subsection{Title block and table of contents}{

The title block appears only if the document has a title, subtitle or author. A table
of contents follows it if the document has sections, unless the document places one
itself.
}

\subsection{Render-time metadata}{

\code{meta} holds information about the act of rendering, e.g. the author of the current
session, the application and its version:
\itemize{
\item It is merged over the document's own metadata: its entries win and \code{NULL} removes
an entry.
\item \code{time} is the render time unless one of them has a time.
\item File name, title block, \code{{trace}}, front matter and post-processors all see the
merged result.
}

Passing this here instead of to the pipeline keeps the layout step from becoming
outdated with every render.
}

\subsection{Error items}{

With \code{on_error = "continue"}, a failed step doesn't stop the pipeline. A layout step
then receives the failure and may place it in the document as an \emph{error item} (see
\code{\link[=pub_doc]{pub_doc()}}). By default, \code{pub_render()} refuses such a document, so that a report
never contains errors. For a final report, therefore, run the pipeline with the
default \code{on_error = "stop"}.

For drafts, you can instead run the pipeline with \code{on_error = "continue"} and set
\code{pub_report_spec(error_items = "show")}. The document is then rendered, with the
error items shown as error boxes and a warning.
}

\subsection{Good to know}{
\itemize{
\item Rendering needs neither the pipeline nor a Shiny session.
\item Every call works in a fresh temporary directory. The file is only written if
everything (including post-processors) succeeded.
}
}

\subsection{Finding Typst}{

Typst is taken from the environment variable \code{PUBFLOW_TYPST} (the typst executable
or its directory), else from the PATH, else from Quarto's installation (found by
\code{QUARTO_PATH} or on the PATH).
}
